nodecode-memobase is a rewrite of fred0m's fred0m/hermes-memobase-provider:

https://github.com/fred0m/hermes-memobase-provider
commit 4e8e447966acefaea372395e2db96ca0d0127f18

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 fred0m (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's recall and profile calls are here a model
tool each, memobase_search and memobase_profile, and the same calls as Lisp
verbs, memobase:search and memobase:profile, plus memobase:remember for the
write the plugin made automatically after every turn. They make the same
requests the Python did -- GET <base>/users/context/<id> with chats_str,
max_token_size, time_range_in_days and fill_window_with_events; GET
<base>/users/profile/<id>; POST <base>/blobs/insert/<id>?wait_process=false
with a chat blob -- with the bearer token in the same header, and the same
user id: uuid5(NAMESPACE_DNS, user_name + "memobase_client"), so this cell
and the original read one store. The original's configuration moves from
environment variables into the cell's `memobase' section, which still reads
MEMOBASE_API_KEY when no token is pasted there. The image's own HTTP call
replaces the requests dependency; a failure is an error carrying
Memobase's own sentence instead of a JSON member.

What was left out: the plugin's hybrid retrieval -- the local BM25, entity
and RRF legs with the temporal boost and the optional cross-encoder rerank
over the returned event window -- and its per-turn lifecycle. The server's
own /users/context assembly answers the recall, and a turn is written back
by the remember verb rather than by a memory-provider hook Nodecode does not
have. The plugin's prefetch caching, circuit breaker and non-primary-agent
write skipping went with it, since each guarded a hot path this cell does
not run.
